## 8. Examples

### 8.1 A Program That Greets a Name from the Command Line

This program demonstrates argument handling, error propagation, and a custom error set:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() !void {
    // General purpose allocator for argument allocation
    var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
    defer _ = gpa.deinit();
    const allocator = gpa.allocator();

    // Obtain owned argument slice
    const args = try std.process.argsAlloc(allocator);
    defer std.process.argsFree(allocator, args);

    const name = if (args.len > 1) args[1] else "World";
    const stdout = std.io.getStdOut().writer();
    try stdout.print("Hello, {s}!\n", .{name});
}
```

Compile and run:

```
$ zig build-exe greet.zig
$ ./greet Alice
Hello, Alice!
$ ./greet
Hello, World!
```

Every allocation is paired with a `defer`; the error from `argsAlloc` propagates cleanly. The allocator’s lifetime is scoped to `main`.

### 8.2 Custom Error Handling at the Boundary

Sometimes you want to catch the error inside `main` and set a specific exit code or message:

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() u8 {
    // Return an exit code directly (0 for success, non-zero for failure)
    if (doWork()) {
        return 0;
    } else |err| {
        std.debug.print("error: {}\n", .{err});
        return 1;
    }
}

fn doWork() !void {
    // Simulate a fallible operation
    return error.SomethingWentWrong;
}
```

Here `main` returns `u8` – an explicit exit code. This bypasses the default error printing and gives full control. Notice that `doWork` still returns `!void`, keeping the error path explicit throughout the call chain.

